        "title": "CrashLoopBackOff",
        "source": "crashloopbackoff.md",
        "section": "Fixes",
        "text": "## Fixes\n\n- If the command is wrong (for example a demo container that ends in `exit 1`), correct `command`/`args` or the image entrypoint so the main process stays in the foreground.\n- If a config value is missing, add the env var or volume and roll the workload.\n- If a dependency is down, fix that service or make startup retry instead of exiting.\n- If a liveness probe is too aggressive, increase `initialDelaySeconds` / `failureThreshold` (see probe-failures).\n- After a fix, roll the deployment and confirm the restart count stops climbing:\n\n  ```bash\n  kubectl rollout status deploy/<name> -n <ns>\n  kubectl get pod <pod> -n <ns> --watch\n  ```",
        "score": 0.7617499582373501,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "Pending and unschedulable pods",
        "source": "pending-unschedulable.md",
        "section": "Diagnosis",
        "text": "## Diagnosis\n\n1. Read the `FailedScheduling` message first \u2014 it usually lists every reason a node was skipped:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl describe pod <pod> -n <ns>\n   kubectl get events -n <ns> --field-selector reason=FailedScheduling\n   ```\n\n2. Compare requests to allocatable resources:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl get pod <pod> -n <ns> -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].resources.requests}'\n   kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,STATUS:.status.conditions[-1],CPU:.status.allocatable.cpu,MEM:.status.allocatable.memory\n   kubectl describe node <node>\n   ```\n\n3. Check taints, affinity, and selectors on the pod against labels and taints on the nodes.\n4. If the message mentions a PVC or volume, inspect the claim (see the PVC pending runbook).",
        "score": 0.7324943804518973,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "CPU throttling",
        "source": "cpu-throttling.md",
        "section": "Diagnosis",
        "text": "## Diagnosis\n\n1. Read the requests and limits:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl get pod <pod> -n <ns> -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].resources}'\n   ```\n\n2. Compare usage with the limit:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl top pod <pod> -n <ns> --containers\n   ```\n\n3. Check throttling in Prometheus:\n\n   ```promql\n   sum by (container) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total{namespace=\"<ns>\", pod=\"<pod>\"}[5m]))\n     / sum by (container) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total{namespace=\"<ns>\", pod=\"<pod>\"}[5m]))\n   ```\n\n4. If a rollout changed the resources, compare revisions with `kubectl rollout history deploy/<name> -n <ns> --revision=<n>`.",
        "score": 0.7301721095979764,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "DNS issues inside the cluster",
        "source": "dns-issues.md",
        "section": "Symptoms",
        "text": "## Symptoms\n\n- Application logs show `unknown host`, `Name or service not known`, `no such host`, `i/o timeout` talking to a Service name, or repeated reconnects to a hostname that should exist.\n- `nslookup` / `getent hosts` / `dig` from a debug container fail for `kubernetes.default` or for the target Service.\n- CoreDNS pods in `kube-system` may be crash-looping, pending, or not ready. Nodes may show `NetworkUnavailable`.\n- Traffic to raw ClusterIPs or pod IPs still works, which is a strong hint the problem is name resolution, not the network path itself.",
        "score": 0.7256078999511076,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "CPU throttling",
        "source": "cpu-throttling.md",
        "section": "Symptoms",
        "text": "## Symptoms\n\n- The pod looks healthy: no restarts, no warning events, no errors in the logs.\n- CPU usage sits at (or just under) the container's CPU limit, for example `20m` used of a `20m` limit.\n- A large share of CFS periods are throttled: `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total / container_cpu_cfs_periods_total` above roughly 25%.\n- Logs show work taking longer than usual (slow batches, slow requests, timeouts to callers), not failing outright.\n- Liveness or readiness probes with tight `timeoutSeconds` may fail intermittently under load.",
        "score": 0.7250773477145471,
        "kind": "runbook"
      }
